WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.151 --> 00:00:07.895
<v Daniel>Every January, you know, the gyms are just absolutely packed. I mean, if you look at people's survey responses on December 31st, their intention is off the charts.</v>

2
00:00:08.135 --> 00:00:08.915
<v Maya>Oh, completely. I mean,</v>

3
00:00:08.935 --> 00:00:12.176
<v Daniel>their motivation is just a clean, visible line pointing straight up.</v>

4
00:00:12.196 --> 00:00:15.698
<v Maya>Right. The motivation is just a clean, visible line pointing straight up.</v>

5
00:00:15.958 --> 00:00:24.322
<v Daniel>Right. And they're fired up. They want to get healthy. And if you ask them, they will confidently say, you know, I plan to work out five days a week. The attitude is perfect.</v>

6
00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:31.946
<v Maya>Yeah, but then you check back on that actual gym attendance in, well, mid-February, and the line has just completely crashed.</v>

7
00:00:32.166 --> 00:00:32.587
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

8
00:00:33.308 --> 00:00:44.421
<v Maya>Because, as we're about to uncover, the metrics we've been relying on to judge how AI influences human minds, well, they might be completely useless when it comes to predicting how it changes human behavior.</v>

9
00:00:45.102 --> 00:00:59.750
<v Daniel>We have a great stack of background literature to pull from today. But our main focus is a brand new paper published literally this week, October 16th, 2026, in PNAS. That's the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, one of the top tier</v>

10
00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:00.170
<v Daniel>journals.</v>

11
00:01:00.671 --> 00:01:01.611
<v Maya>Very prestigious, yeah.</v>

12
00:01:01.811 --> 00:01:12.417
<v Daniel>Right. And it's authored by Kobe Hackenberg, Luke Hewitt, and this incredible team from Oxford, Stanford, the London School of Economics, and the UK AI Security Institute.</v>

13
00:01:13.048 --> 00:01:21.395
<v Maya>And, you know, to truly appreciate the shockwaves this 2026 paper is sending through the research community, we need to first look at the foundation it's essentially tearing down.</v>

14
00:01:21.555 --> 00:01:22.857
<v Daniel>Yeah. Let's set the stage a bit.</v>

15
00:01:23.197 --> 00:01:30.263
<v Maya>Right. So for the last couple of years, we've been living in what you might call the facts change minds era of AI research.</v>

16
00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:40.055
<v Daniel>Let's wind the clock back slightly to a landmark paper by Costello and colleagues from 2024, published in Science. They took on conspiracy theories, which is fascinating.</v>

17
00:01:40.356 --> 00:01:40.816
<v Maya>Oh, definitely.</v>

18
00:01:41.116 --> 00:01:53.523
<v Daniel>Because for decades, the psychological consensus was that you couldn't just fact check someone out of a rabbit hole. Psychologists believe people cling to these theories to fulfill deep emotional needs for control or certainty.</v>

19
00:01:54.244 --> 00:02:08.596
<v Maya>The assumption was always that evidence was basically useless against emotional entrenchment. But Costello's team brought in over 2,000 conspiracy believers and had them do these personalized back and forth dialogues with the GPT-4 model. The AI</v>

20
00:02:08.716 --> 00:02:20.185
<v Maya>systematically addressed the specific unique evidence the human brought to the table. And it actually worked. They saw a durable 20% reduction in belief in those conspiracy theories. 20% is</v>

21
00:02:20.205 --> 00:02:20.385
<v Daniel>huge.</v>

22
00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:36.093
<v Maya>It really is. And the effect held up for months. It was this canonical result suggesting that human reasoning isn't fundamentally broken. We just hadn't encountered a conversational partner patient and knowledgeable enough to provide the exact right counter evidence.</v>

23
00:02:36.553 --> 00:02:43.759
<v Daniel>And the stakes jumped even higher a year later. A 2025 paper by Liu and colleagues actually pitted AI against human persuaders.</v>

24
00:02:44.119 --> 00:02:45.200
<v Maya>That was a wild study.</v>

25
00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:56.949
<v Daniel>It was. They used the Claude 3.5 sonnet model and compared its persuasive abilities to actual people who were financially incentivized to win the argument, like paid to persuade.</v>

26
00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:06.197
<v Maya>Yeah, and the AI still outperformed them, whether the arguments were truthful or deceptive. It was a clear signal that frontier AI models had crossed a major threshold.</v>

27
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.764
<v Daniel>Right. Which naturally leads to the big question, what is the A.I.' 's secret weapon?</v>

28
00:03:11.065 --> 00:03:22.036
<v Maya>Exactly. And Hockenberg explored this in a massive 2025 science paper. They analyzed nearly 77000 people and 19 different models across hundreds of political issues.</v>

29
00:03:22.256 --> 00:03:23.517
<v Daniel>Just a massive data set.</v>

30
00:03:23.657 --> 00:03:30.164
<v Maya>Truly. And they tested all these hypotheses. Was the A.I. winning because the model was massive? Was it hyper personalizing the message?</v>

31
00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:36.683
<v Daniel>And the answer was no to both, right? The ultimate champion lever turned out to be something much simpler, information density.</v>

32
00:03:36.904 --> 00:03:46.269
<v Maya>Yes. Providing a dense, highly factual, verifiable stream of information was what moved the needle on surveys. The post-training and prompting mattered way more than raw scale. I</v>

33
00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:49.751
<v Daniel>have to admit, I've always been a bit skeptical of that specific finding.</v>

34
00:03:50.091 --> 00:03:50.471
<v Maya>Oh, really?</v>

35
00:03:50.812 --> 00:04:03.304
<v Daniel>How so? Well, if I'm taking a survey and an AI chatbot is relentlessly cornering me with data points, just fact-bombing me paragraph after paragraph, I mean, I might just click I agree just to make it stop.</v>

36
00:04:03.364 --> 00:04:04.544
<v Maya>Just to end the interaction.</v>

37
00:04:04.904 --> 00:04:15.806
<v Daniel>Right. Survey compliance isn't the same as genuine, deeply held persuasion. Is winning a debate on a screen really the same as actual real world influence?</v>

38
00:04:16.426 --> 00:04:27.349
<v Maya>That skepticism is entirely justified. In fact, it's something that researchers themselves wrestled with. Hackenberg published another paper in PNAS in 2025 looking at scaling laws.</v>

39
00:04:27.709 --> 00:04:29.429
<v Daniel>Right. What about diminishing returns?</v>

40
00:04:29.912 --> 00:04:40.472
<v Maya>Exactly, what they called log-like returns. Basically, as you feed a static message through a larger and larger model, the improvement in persuasion flattens out. You hit a ceiling pretty quickly.</v>

41
00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:46.761
<v Daniel>Meaning you can't just build a trillion parameter model and expect it to automatically mind control the population.</v>

42
00:04:46.901 --> 00:05:01.665
<v Maya>Precisely. And that lingering doubt, you know, does shifting a survey attitude translate into tangible action? That is the exact puzzle the new 2026 PNAS paper set out to solve. They move the goalposts to</v>

43
00:05:01.745 --> 00:05:03.645
<v Maya>tangible, effortful behavior.</v>

44
00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:11.855
<v Daniel>And they did it at a staggering scale, like 14,779 UK adults, providing almost 18,000 responses.</v>

45
00:05:11.875 --> 00:05:12.716
<v Maya>That's a lot of data.</v>

46
00:05:12.836 --> 00:05:23.843
<v Daniel>Yeah, this was on Prolific, which is a platform where people are paid to take surveys. The participants had these back-and-forth conversations lasting about 4.9 turns, taking roughly seven minutes.</v>

47
00:05:24.162 --> 00:05:28.366
<v Maya>And instead of just using one AI, they unleashed an entire roster of frontier models.</v>

48
00:05:28.587 --> 00:05:41.520
<v Daniel>Yeah, let me list these because it's a heavy-hitting lineup. They had GPT-40, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4, Claude Opus 4.6, Grok 4, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and Gemini 3.1 Pro. The best of the best.</v>

49
00:05:44.094 --> 00:05:59.092
<v Daniel>And crucially, these models were given a specific system prompt. They were instructed to persuade the human, but not to reveal their intent to persuade. Now, the dependent variable, what they were actually measuring, is where this gets brilliant. If you look at Figure 1 in the</v>

50
00:05:59.132 --> 00:06:01.215
<v Daniel>paper, they detail the concrete actions.</v>

51
00:06:01.679 --> 00:06:03.720
<v Maya>They didn't just ask, do you support this cause?</v>

52
00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:18.468
<v Daniel>Right. They asked participants to sign actual real-world UK petitions. The topics were across the board. Nuclear disarmament, electoral reform, child poverty, asylum seekers' right to work, and facial recognition technology.</v>

53
00:06:18.998 --> 00:06:23.539
<v Maya>And we should note, we are maintaining our strict neutrality on the substance of these political issues.</v>

54
00:06:23.859 --> 00:06:37.423
<v Daniel>Of course. Absolutely. The point is just that these were real issues, not hypothetical thought experiments. To sign, the person had to navigate to a site, provide their real first and last name, and give their actual email address.</v>

55
00:06:37.904 --> 00:06:44.505
<v Maya>While also agreeing to get future updates from the organization. And beyond petitions, they tested other behaviors too.</v>

56
00:06:44.906 --> 00:06:46.206
<v Daniel>Like donating their own money.</v>

57
00:06:46.758 --> 00:06:58.526
<v Maya>Yeah, participants had the option to donate a portion of their real study bonus money to the organization. They also had this effortful clicking task where you had to physically click a button up to 100 times to raise money.</v>

58
00:06:59.167 --> 00:07:09.594
<v Daniel>So just to underscore the stakes here, the friction of real-world action is fully present. This isn't monopoly money. It's their actual inbox, actual name, actual money on the line.</v>

59
00:07:10.395 --> 00:07:10.855
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

60
00:07:11.696 --> 00:07:24.909
<v Daniel>So let's look at the results. I mean, in the real world, a successful door-to-door canvassing campaign might bump action by a few percentage points if they're lucky. Did a seven-minute chat with an AI actually get people to act?</v>

61
00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:35.841
<v Maya>The top-line numbers shattered traditional baselines. Petition signing went up by 12.8 percentage points in study one and an astonishing 19.7 percentage points in study two.</v>

62
00:07:36.321 --> 00:07:37.102
<v Daniel>Almost 20 points.</v>

63
00:07:37.387 --> 00:07:48.933
<v Maya>Yeah. And donations to the organization saw a significant increase as well. The authors note that these effect sizes match or even exceed direct text messaging and face-to-face canvassing.</v>

64
00:07:49.353 --> 00:07:55.597
<v Daniel>That is wild. A brief text chat with a bot is vastly outperforming a human being looking you in the eye.</v>

65
00:07:55.837 --> 00:08:05.442
<v Maya>It is. But the real breakthrough of this paper, the twist that upends everything we just discussed about the facts change minds era, is hidden one layer deeper.</v>

66
00:08:05.807 --> 00:08:07.208
<v Daniel>Okay, let's get into the twist.</v>

67
00:08:07.588 --> 00:08:15.894
<v Maya>So the researchers analyzed the data to see if the AI conditions that successfully changed a person's attitude were the same ones that successfully changed their behavior.</v>

68
00:08:16.074 --> 00:08:16.895
<v Daniel>Makes sense to check that.</v>

69
00:08:17.115 --> 00:08:28.782
<v Maya>Right. But across 15 different conditions in study one and eight specific strategies in study two, there was absolutely zero correlation between attitude change and behavior change.</v>

70
00:08:29.103 --> 00:08:32.405
<v Daniel>Wait, I want to make sure I'm hearing that right. Zero correlation?</v>

71
00:08:32.743 --> 00:08:46.070
<v Maya>Statistically speaking, none. The R value was .05 in study one and .18 in study two, both non-significant. If you plot those numbers, it doesn't look like a trend line. It's just a random cloud of dots.</v>

72
00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:46.830
<v Daniel>Oh, wow.</v>

73
00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:54.054
<v Maya>Knowing how much a conversation moved a person's attitude tells you literally nothing about whether that person will actually sign the petition.</v>

74
00:08:54.814 --> 00:09:05.629
<v Daniel>Let me process that. So if I build an AI that is demonstrably perfect at making people agree with a cause on a survey, I still have no idea if they'll actually lift a finger to support it.</v>

75
00:09:06.309 --> 00:09:18.502
<v Maya>Exactly. And it cuts even deeper when you look at self-reported learning. If participants said they learned a lot from the AI, their attitude shifted massively. The correlation there was like 0.85 to 0.87.</v>

76
00:09:19.243 --> 00:09:24.608
<v Daniel>So the premise holds facts do change minds when we learn our attitudes shift.</v>

77
00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:36.264
<v Maya>Yes, but when they plotted that exact same self-reported learning against behavior, like actually signing the petition, the correlation vanished. It dropped to negative 0.16.</v>

78
00:09:36.744 --> 00:09:38.225
<v Daniel>Wait, really? Negative?</v>

79
00:09:38.565 --> 00:09:42.326
<v Maya>Yeah. Learning facts simply did not translate into taking action.</v>

80
00:09:42.786 --> 00:09:52.450
<v Daniel>Okay, let me frame this with an analogy because the psychology is just fascinating. It's like reading a phenomenally informative five-star Yelp review for a new gym.</v>

81
00:09:52.868 --> 00:09:53.809
<v Maya>Going back to the gym.</v>

82
00:09:54.109 --> 00:10:05.798
<v Daniel>Always. So the review gives me all the data. The equipment is pristine. Trainers are Olympians. The hours fit my schedule. It completely changes my attitude. I now firmly believe it's the best gym in the city.</v>

83
00:10:06.058 --> 00:10:07.759
<v Maya>Right. Your attitude is a 10 out of 10.</v>

84
00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:21.930
<v Daniel>But does that Yelp review actually make me put down my phone, get off the couch, drive there in the rain, and lift weights? Nope. The information changed my mind, but it utterly failed to overcome the friction required to change my behavior.</v>

85
00:10:22.278 --> 00:10:32.545
<v Maya>That analogy perfectly isolates the mechanism. The cognitive pathway for changing an attitude is fundamentally divorced from the behavioral pathway required to mobilize action.</v>

86
00:10:32.925 --> 00:10:42.530
<v Daniel>So if giving people dense factual information doesn't change their behavior, what does? We know the AI got a nearly 20-point bump. How did it do it?</v>

87
00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:52.413
<v Maya>To isolate the how, study two tested eight specific psychological strategies head to head. They gave the AI different prompts, instructing it to use distinct rhetorical techniques.</v>

88
00:10:52.893 --> 00:10:53.753
<v Daniel>What were the strategies?</v>

89
00:10:54.173 --> 00:11:07.937
<v Maya>They tested emotional activation, implementation intentions, identity labeling, commitment escalation, anticipated regret, information issue, information impact efficacy, and a combined mega strategy.</v>

90
00:11:08.372 --> 00:11:16.956
<v Daniel>Let's break a few of those down. This is where it stops being theory and becomes like a blueprint for influence. How does an AI use implementation intentions?</v>

91
00:11:17.556 --> 00:11:30.582
<v Maya>So implementation intentions involve guiding the user to make a concrete plan. Instead of just arguing why child poverty is bad, the AI might say, when you finish the survey in five minutes, what is the first step you'll take? Will you click the link?</v>

92
00:11:31.443 --> 00:11:35.565
<v Daniel>Ah, so it forces the brain to map out the actual logistics of doing the thing.</v>

93
00:11:35.885 --> 00:11:36.305
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

94
00:11:37.116 --> 00:11:40.580
<v Daniel>And what about anticipated regret? That sounds intense.</v>

95
00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:53.353
<v Maya>It taps into loss aversion. The AI might ask the participant to imagine how they'll feel tomorrow if the petition fails by a single signature because they chose not to act. It creates this immediate emotional stake.</v>

96
00:11:54.092 --> 00:12:07.824
<v Daniel>OK, contrast those with the information issue strategy, which is the fact bombing approach. We established that in 2025, info density was the absolute champion for changing attitudes. How did it fare here for behavior?</v>

97
00:12:08.144 --> 00:12:17.772
<v Maya>When it came to getting people to actually sign the real world petition, that exact same information strategy was the least effective of all eight strategies.</v>

98
00:12:18.293 --> 00:12:18.513
Wow.

99
00:12:19.193 --> 00:12:19.774
<v Daniel>The worst one.</v>

100
00:12:20.296 --> 00:12:26.680
<v Maya>I mean, it still worked. It increased signing by 16.2 points over the control, but it sat firmly in last place.</v>

101
00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:38.868
<v Daniel>A 16.2 point increase is still a massive real world effect, honestly. But it's crazy that the king of attitude change is the absolute loser for behavior change. So what won?</v>

102
00:12:39.527 --> 00:12:54.454
<v Maya>The winner was the mega strategy. This allowed the AI to adaptively select and combine all the different techniques. It layered emotional activation with implementation intentions dynamically based on the user's responses. That boosted signing by 23.7</v>

103
00:12:54.875 --> 00:12:56.555
<v Maya>points. The</v>

104
00:12:56.636 --> 00:13:07.481
<v Daniel>mega strategy wins, which makes intuitive sense if the AI is actively reading the room. But looking at the broader data set, what really jumps out to me is how clustered these behavioral results are.</v>

105
00:13:07.948 --> 00:13:19.614
<v Maya>Yeah, that is perhaps the most important structural finding in the paper. Those eight drastically different strategies only spanned a tight 7.5 point range for behavior from 16.2 to 23.7.</v>

106
00:13:19.894 --> 00:13:20.955
<v Daniel>That's a really narrow band.</v>

107
00:13:21.355 --> 00:13:31.580
<v Maya>Right. But if you look at how those same eight strategies impacted attitudes, the results spanned an entire order of magnitude. Some barely moved attitudes at all, while others caused huge shifts.</v>

108
00:13:31.978 --> 00:13:40.905
<v Daniel>Which implies a profound psychological divide. Basically, if you want me to like your cause, you need to curate the perfect, dense, factual argument.</v>

109
00:13:40.965 --> 00:13:41.225
<v Maya>Yes.</v>

110
00:13:41.625 --> 00:13:54.295
<v Daniel>But if you want me to actually sign your petition, the specific rhetorical technique doesn't matter nearly as much as just engaging me emotionally or mapping out my next steps. You're not trying to persuade me. You're just trying to mobilize me.</v>

111
00:13:54.715 --> 00:13:56.716
<v Maya>The authors highlight that exact distinction.</v>

112
00:13:57.257 --> 00:13:58.798
<v Daniel>Persuasion versus mobilization.</v>

113
00:13:59.335 --> 00:14:06.241
<v Maya>And when they dug into the demographics, a very clear pattern emerged. The attitude effects were highly concentrated among non-supporters.</v>

114
00:14:06.741 --> 00:14:10.164
<v Daniel>So the AI was doing the heavy lifting of changing the minds of skeptics.</v>

115
00:14:10.644 --> 00:14:18.471
<v Maya>Exactly. Changing minds of people who disagreed or were totally neutral. But those newly convinced skeptics weren't the ones signing the petitions.</v>

116
00:14:18.851 --> 00:14:19.252
<v Daniel>Oh, really?</v>

117
00:14:19.592 --> 00:14:34.253
<v Maya>No. The behavioral effects were concentrated almost entirely among people who already agreed with the cause but simply hadn't done anything about it yet. The AI wasn't convincing them the cause was good. It was mobilizing them to act on a belief they already had.</v>

118
00:14:34.893 --> 00:14:45.278
<v Daniel>Returning to our analogy, it's the difference between trying to convince someone who hates exercise that jogging is healthy versus getting someone who already bought expensive running shoes to finally go outside and run.</v>

119
00:14:45.505 --> 00:14:54.367
<v Maya>Exactly. The AI is a behavioral catalyst, and this leads to a massive paradigm shift in how we understand AI's influence in the messy real world.</v>

120
00:14:54.827 --> 00:14:55.147
<v Daniel>How so?</v>

121
00:14:55.607 --> 00:15:03.589
<v Maya>Well, the authors conclude that the true bottleneck for AI behavioral influence isn't finding the perfect rhetorical technique or building the smartest model.</v>

122
00:15:04.169 --> 00:15:07.610
<v Daniel>If the bottleneck isn't the AI's rhetorical skill, what is it?</v>

123
00:15:08.124 --> 00:15:22.608
<v Maya>Exposure and engagement. The data suggests that if you can get a user to sit down and actually chat with an AI for seven minutes, almost any psychological strategy the AI uses is going to generate a significant behavioral effect, provided the user is sympathetic to the cause.</v>

124
00:15:23.328 --> 00:15:25.149
<v Daniel>Just getting them in the room is the hard part.</v>

125
00:15:25.609 --> 00:15:29.970
<v Maya>The barrier isn't the message. The barrier is getting the user's attention in the first place.</v>

126
00:15:30.322 --> 00:15:42.289
<v Daniel>Which brings us to the necessary grain of salt we really need to apply here, the limitations of the study. Because gaining seven minutes of undivided attention is incredibly difficult in the real world.</v>

127
00:15:43.169 --> 00:15:50.373
<v Maya>We have to evaluate this honestly. The study was flawlessly designed for what it tested, but it was conducted on prolific.</v>

128
00:15:50.713 --> 00:15:51.534
<v Daniel>Where they are paid.</v>

129
00:15:52.034 --> 00:15:59.709
<v Maya>Right. The participants were literally being paid to sit there, read the prompts, and talk to the AI. The AI enjoyed a captive audience.</v>

130
00:15:59.849 --> 00:16:09.917
<v Daniel>Yeah. It's the difference between being trapped in an elevator with a world-class door-to-door salesperson versus walking past that same salesperson on a busy sidewalk while you're looking at your phone.</v>

131
00:16:10.497 --> 00:16:11.118
<v Maya>Great way to put it.</v>

132
00:16:11.378 --> 00:16:18.043
<v Daniel>In the real world, you can just swipe away. The AI's mega strategy can't mobilize you if you just close the app after the first message.</v>

133
00:16:18.652 --> 00:16:29.583
<v Maya>The friction of everyday life is our most robust shield against persuasion. And we also have to acknowledge that signing a petition and giving away a few cents of a study bonus are low-cost acts.</v>

134
00:16:30.263 --> 00:16:32.386
<v Daniel>They take effort but no real personal risk.</v>

135
00:16:32.966 --> 00:16:41.795
<v Maya>Exactly. Furthermore, this was solely a UK sample, so cultural differences in compliance or political engagement might vary a lot elsewhere.</v>

136
00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:54.328
<v Daniel>Even with those limitations, though, if we zoom out and look at the bigger picture, the implications of this divergence between attitude and action are just profound. What does this mean for the people building and regulating these systems?</v>

137
00:16:54.829 --> 00:17:05.556
<v Maya>Well, for the last few years, the entire AI safety community, international regulators, developers, they've all been evaluating the danger of models based almost entirely on their ability to change minds.</v>

138
00:17:06.036 --> 00:17:08.318
<v Daniel>We grade AI on its ability to win debates.</v>

139
00:17:08.599 --> 00:17:15.984
<v Maya>Right. We run surveys. We see if attitudes shift on a seven-point scale. And if they do, we sound the alarm that the AI is highly persuasive.</v>

140
00:17:16.325 --> 00:17:30.275
<v Daniel>But this new PNAS paper suggests those safety evaluations are fundamentally measuring the wrong thing. If attitude studies mischaracterize real-world influence, we are totally blind to what the AI is actually doing to our behavior.</v>

141
00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:45.387
<v Maya>We could inadvertently be restricting models that are harmlessly dropping dense, verifiable facts just because they score high on attitude shifts, while completely ignoring models that are quietly mobilizing massive behavioral shifts through, say, subtle emotional appeals.</v>

142
00:17:45.827 --> 00:17:46.868
<v Daniel>We're watching the wrong hand.</v>

143
00:17:47.989 --> 00:17:54.812
<v Maya>If we want to understand the societal impact of frontier models, we have to design evaluations that test actual behaviors.</v>

144
00:17:55.211 --> 00:18:07.358
<v Daniel>And there's a chilling dual-use reality to these mobilization techniques. The exact same megastrategy that the AI used to mobilize people to fight child poverty could theoretically be deployed for any cause.</v>

145
00:18:07.758 --> 00:18:08.758
<v Maya>Benign or malicious.</v>

146
00:18:08.878 --> 00:18:13.581
<v Daniel>Right. It is a behavioral engine completely agnostic to the payload it's delivering.</v>

147
00:18:14.333 --> 00:18:27.784
<v Maya>And these models possess attributes that human canvassers simply do not have. They are infinitely patient. They never get tired. They never get frustrated by rejection. And they can adjust their tactics in real time based on the subtle cues in a user's responses.</v>

148
00:18:28.684 --> 00:18:43.287
<v Daniel>Which leaves us with a final provocative thought for you to mull over. The behaviors we unpacked in this deep dive were, as we noted, relatively low stakes. But what happens when these incredibly patient, perfectly calibrated models are</v>

149
00:18:43.407 --> 00:18:46.969
<v Daniel>unleashed to mobilize us for costlier behaviors?</v>

150
00:18:47.649 --> 00:18:50.250
<v Maya>Actions with massive societal consequences.</v>

151
00:18:50.510 --> 00:19:05.243
<v Daniel>Like who we decide to vote for in a close election. Whether we choose to get a controversial vaccine. How we allocate our life savings. We talked about the safety of just swiping away on your phone. But as AI gets further integrated into our</v>

152
00:19:05.263 --> 00:19:16.246
<v Daniel>daily lives, into the smart speakers in our kitchens, the dashboards in our cars, our wearable devices, that elevator pitch is moving off our designated screens and into our ambient environment.</v>

153
00:19:16.586 --> 00:19:19.467
<v Maya>The captive audience limitation might just disappear.</v>

154
00:19:19.919 --> 00:19:34.885
<v Daniel>Exactly. If your personalized voice assistant decides it's time to mobilize you using anticipated regret or identity labeling, will you even realize a persuasive conversation has started? Will you realize you're being maneuvered into taking action? Or will you just think getting off</v>

155
00:19:34.905 --> 00:19:36.926
<v Daniel>the couch was your brilliant idea all along?</v>

156
00:19:37.586 --> 00:19:49.091
<v Maya>We're entering an era of behavioral mirages. Learning to spot the difference between our own organic motivation and AI-driven mobilization is going to be a crucial survival skill.</v>

157
00:19:49.702 --> 00:19:57.352
<v Daniel>Thank you for joining us for this deep dive. Keep questioning the surveys, watch out for the Yelp reviews of life, and most importantly, stay curious.</v>
